course: physics
lesson: 13
title: Water safety and the ocean
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The deeper you go in water, the harder it presses, because more and more water is piled up above you; you feel it in your ears at the bottom of a deep pool.
explain: That is why dams are thick at the bottom, why divers come up slowly, and why deep-sea fish, full of water that hardly squashes, are built for the crushing deep.
explain: Water also holds up most of your weight, so a friend feels light in the pool, and a boat stays steady only while its weight is kept low.
explain: Knowing how water behaves keeps people safe: thin ice, rip currents and a person quietly struggling in the water are real dangers, and the safe move is always to call an adult or a lifeguard.
fact: Thick, very cold ice can carry a heavy lorry across a frozen lake in the far north, but ice on ponds at home can look solid and be far too thin, so never walk on it.
fact: Every 10 metres of seawater presses as hard as the whole air above us, so divers' ears hurt as they go deeper.
fact: Divers equalise their ears (even out the push inside them, for example by pinching the nose and blowing gently) as they go down, and come up slowly, because shooting up fast can badly hurt them.
fact: Deep-sea fish live under enormous pressure because their bodies are full of water, which hardly squashes and is pressed evenly from all sides.
fact: Some deep-sea fish brought up quickly are hurt because gas inside them grows.
fact: A dam is thick at the bottom because water presses harder the deeper it is.
fact: A person drowning often cannot wave or shout; they bob, gasping, with the head tilted back, because all their effort goes into breathing.
fact: If someone in the water looks wrong, call an adult or a lifeguard at once and never jump in after them yourself.
fact: A rip current is water piled up by waves rushing back out to sea through a narrow channel.
fact: If a rip current pulls you out, swim sideways along the beach, not against it, or float and wave for help.
fact: A canoe tips over if someone stands up suddenly, because a boat is steady only while its weight is low, so keep low and in the middle.
fact: Polystyrene, the light white foam used in packing, is mostly trapped air bubbles, so it floats almost on top of the water, and bits of it pollute the sea for years.
fact: A goldfish stays at one depth by keeping just the right amount of gas in its swim bladder, and a fish floating on its side may have a sick swim bladder.
fact: In a pool the water holds up most of your weight, because our bodies are about as heavy as water for their size, which makes swimming good for sore joints.
exercise: Why is a frozen pond dangerous even if it looks solid? | The ice can be far too thin to hold you | Ice is always too slippery to stand on | Frozen ponds are full of hidden fish | The ice gets thicker when you stand on it
exercise: How can a heavy lorry cross a frozen lake in the far north? | The ice there is very thick and very cold | Lorries are lighter than people when they are on ice | The lorry floats on the water underneath | The wheels melt a road in the ice
exercise: Why does deep water squeeze your ears? | Water presses harder the deeper you go | The water is much colder and that hurts your ears | Your ears slowly fill up with salt from the sea | Fish make very loud noises down there in the dark
exercise: How hard does every 10 metres of seawater press? | As hard as the whole air above us | As hard as a single feather resting on you | Not at all, because water does not press | As hard as the whole Earth pressing down
exercise: What do divers do to stay safe? | Equalise their ears and come up slowly | Swim back up to the top as fast as they can | Hold their breath all the way up | Dive straight down with no stops
exercise: How can fish live at the bottom of the ocean? | Their bodies are full of water, which hardly squashes | They have shells as hard as steel | There is no pressure at the bottom | They keep swimming up to rest
exercise: Why are some deep-sea fish hurt when brought up quickly? | Gas inside them grows | The light is too bright for them | They get too cold near the top | They become too heavy to swim
exercise: Why are dams thick at the bottom? | Water presses harder lower down | The bottom holds up the road on top | Fish need thick walls to live in | It is cheaper to build that way
exercise: What does a person in real trouble in the water often look like? | Bobbing and gasping, head tilted back, unable to shout | Waving both arms and shouting loudly | Swimming fast towards the beach | Lying still and calm on their back
exercise: What should you do if someone in the water looks wrong? | Call an adult or a lifeguard at once | Jump in after them yourself | Wait and watch them quietly for a few more minutes | Throw stones to wake them up
exercise: What is a rip current? | Water from the waves rushing back out through a narrow channel | A big wave that rolls onto the beach | A warm current from far-off seas | A whirlpool caused by a ship
exercise: What should you do if a current pulls you out to sea? | Swim sideways along the beach, or float and wave | Swim straight back against it as hard as you can | Dive down to the bottom and wait | Take off your swimsuit to be lighter
exercise: Why must you stay low in a canoe? | A boat is steady only while its weight is low | Standing makes the canoe heavier | The paddle only works when sitting | Waves are always much higher when you are standing up
exercise: Why does polystyrene float so high? | It is mostly trapped air bubbles | It is made of a very light kind of wood | It is pushed up by the waves underneath | It is magnetic, so the air pulls it up
exercise: How does a goldfish stay at one depth? | Its swim bladder holds just the right amount of gas | It flaps its fins all the time | It holds onto water plants | It drinks water to stay down
exercise: Why is it easy to lift someone in the pool? | The water holds up most of their weight | People are much lighter when their skin is wet | Pool water is full of air | The bottom of the pool pushes up
exercise: Why is swimming good exercise for people with sore joints? | The water holds up most of the body's weight | Water warms the joints like a heater | Swimming uses no muscles at all | Pool water has medicine in it
test: My mate reckons drowning people always scream and wave, so you'd know. right? | No, they often cannot shout or wave at all | Yes, they always scream for help | Yes, and they always splash a lot so everyone sees | No, they always swim fast
test: the pond's frozen solid, dare ya to walk across. do it? | No, the ice can be too thin even if it looks solid | Yes, frozen means safe | Yes, if you walk fast | Yes, if you take off your shoes
test: You are caught in a rip current. Your friend says swim hard for the shore. What is better? | Swim sideways along the beach or float and wave | Swim even harder straight back | Dive under the water | Stop moving and sink
test: You see a kid in the deep end bobbing and gasping. What do you do? | Shout for the lifeguard at once | Jump in and grab them yourself straight away | Laugh, because they are only playing a game | Walk away and say nothing to anyone
test: Your sister stands up in a canoe to wave. What will probably happen? | The canoe tips over | The canoe goes faster | The canoe sinks slowly | Nothing at all
test: What would happen to a diver who shot up from deep water very fast? | They could get badly hurt | Nothing, it is the safest way | They would float forever | They would get heavier
test: In a cartoon, a dam is built thin at the bottom and thick at the top. What is wrong? | The water presses hardest at the bottom | Nothing, because that is the right shape for a dam | Water presses hardest at the top | Dams do not hold any water
test: Your goldfish floats on its side. What might be wrong? | Its swim bladder may be sick | It is just having a nap on its side | It has eaten far too much fish food | The water in the tank is too clean
test: Why is polystyrene in the sea a problem? | It floats for years and pollutes the sea | It sinks and blocks the seabed | It melts in the sun and heats up the sea water | It makes the waves bigger
test: Why can you easily lift a heavy friend in the pool but not on land? | Water holds up most of their weight | Your arms get stronger in water | Your friend breathes out all the air | Wet skin is easier to grip
test: Why can't people swim down to where the deepest fish live? | The water presses far too hard down there | It is far too dark down there to see where to swim | The fish would chase them | The water is too salty
test: Why does a lorry not fall through the ice of a frozen northern lake? | The ice is thick and very cold | The lorry is driven very fast | The lorry has no weight on ice | The water below holds the lorry
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pressure exerted by water at increasing depth? | It increases with the depth of water above | It diminishes with the depth of water above | It remains uniform at all depths | It is exerted only upon the surface
exam: Identify the conduct appropriate to a bather who is carried seawards by a rip current. | Swimming parallel to the shore, or floating and signalling for assistance | Swimming directly against the current with full exertion | Descending to the seabed until the current subsides | Removing all clothing so as to diminish weight
exam: It is given that a person in the water is observed to bob and gasp with the head inclined backwards. Ascertain the appropriate course of action for a child observer. | To summon an adult or lifeguard without delay | To enter the water and effect a rescue in person | To await further signals of distress | To withdraw and inform no person
exam: Determine the reason for which a dam is constructed with greater thickness at its base. | The pressure of the water increases with depth | The base must support a carriageway | Fish require a thick wall for shelter | The upper portion of the dam bears the greatest pressure
